.TH GIT-REVIEW 1 "2026-08-30" "git-review" "Git Extensions"
.SH NAME
git-review \- offline-first code review stored in the git object store
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B git review
.RI [ command ]
.RI [ args ]
.br
.B git review
.RI [ id " [" comment ]]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B git review
tracks change proposals inside the repository itself. A review is a base, a
head, commentary anchored to the code, verdicts from the people who read it,
and a terminal state of merged or abandoned. It is stored as git objects under
dedicated refs, alongside the code, and syncs the way code does.
.PP
A pull request is a review with an entry in the origin ledger; a review of a
local branch \(em by a person or by an agent \(em is one that has none. There
is no second type for that case and no flag telling them apart, and every
command works the same on both.
.PP
With no arguments it lists reviews; with an id it shows that review. Those are
shorthands for
.B list
and
.BR show .
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.B add
Open a new review. With no
.B --base
and
.BR --head ,
both come from where you are standing: the head is the branch you are on,
qualified with its remote when it has one, and the base is that remote's
default branch.
.TP
.B list
List reviews, with filters for state, base branch, draft, checks, label,
reviewer and author. On a terminal the listing nests under each review's
parent; down a pipe it prints flat and in words rather than glyphs.
.TP
.B show
Print one review in full: its branches and revision, each person's current
verdict, the head commit's check runs, and every thread \(em where in the code
it sits, whether it is resolved, and whether its anchor still describes the
head. A second id prints one thread.
.TP
.B status
What is standing between the review and a merge. Exits 0 when nothing is and 1
when something is, so a script can branch on it.
.B --json
gives an agent the same answer with a shape.
.TP
.B checks
The check runs recorded against a commit.
.B --set
records one.
.TP
.B diff
The change the review proposes, computed from base\(embhead. Every
.BR git-diff (1)
flag passes through.
.TP
.B checkout
Fetch the review's head and check it out, from the fork it lives in where that
is not a remote here. With no id, check out the repository's default branch; with
a second id, open the file that comment is anchored to at its line, using
.B review.openCommand
where the editor is one this build does not know how to point at a line.
.TP
.B comment
Comment on a review.
.B --on
.IR path : line
anchors it to a place in the code; a second id replies in that thread.
.TP
.B edit
Change a review's fields, or rewrite one comment.
.B --sync
brings the recorded revision up to the head branch as it stands now.
.TP
.B close
Close the review \(em abandoned, not merged. With a second id it resolves one
thread instead.
.TP
.B reopen
Reopen the review, or unresolve one thread.
.TP
.B approve
.TQ
.B request-changes
Record a verdict. It carries the revision it was cast against, so a later push
makes it stale.
.TP
.B remove
Unlist the review, or retract one comment.
.TP
.B log
The tracker's own history, one commit per action.
.TP
.B pull
.TQ
.B push
Sync with another clone or with a forge. A bare remote name moves the notes
refs and the origin ledger;
.B github:
reads and writes pull requests through the GitHub bridge, and
.B ado:
does the same through the Azure DevOps bridge. Either bridge target's URL must
name the repository. See
.BR "THE TWO LEGS" .
.SH THE POSITIONAL SLOTS
One positional is the review; two are a comment inside it. That slot is the
same for
.BR show ", " edit ", " remove ", " close " and " reopen ,
so
.B close
.I id
closes the review and
.B close
.I id comment
resolves that thread. Comment ids abbreviate like object names, and the prefix
only has to be unique within the review.
.SH ANCHORS
A review comment's anchor records the path, the lines and
.I the revision it was written against
\(em never just the path. Two distinct things then make it stop describing the
head, and they are reported as the different things they are:
.TP
.B outdated
The anchor's commit is still reachable, but the file under it changed. This is
the ordinary case after a plain push. Note that ancestry alone is not the test:
an anchor's commit stays an ancestor across every non-rewriting push, so a
client testing reachability would call a thread current long after the lines
under it were replaced.
.TP
.B detached
The anchor's commit is not reachable from the head \(em a rebase or an amend
\(em and it may not be in this object store at all.
.PP
Neither is ever repaired by moving the anchor. An anchor is part of what
somebody said, and relocating it would edit their words to point somewhere they
never looked.
.SH VERDICTS
A verdict is one person's position, and it supersedes their previous one rather
than retracting it: both events stay in the blob, so the record that somebody
once asked for changes survives. A verdict cast against an older revision is
.BR stale ,
shown as stale and never dropped or quietly re-pointed at the new head.
.PP
Verdicts are advisory. Nothing in a grow-only set can prevent a merge; upstream
that is branch protection's job, and here
.B status
reports it and stops.
.SH CHECKS
Build results, quality gates and scans live on
.B refs/notes/checks/runs
.RI "\(em keyed by " "commit sha" ", not by review."
A check result is a fact about a commit: true whichever review contains it,
shared by every branch that contains it, and outliving the review. That keying
is also what makes rebases behave with no logic \(em an old commit keeps its
old results, and a rewritten one is a different key with none yet \(em and it
is why
.B git review checks --commit HEAD --set build=fail
works with no review involved.
.PP
.B git log --notes=checks/runs
renders them against the commits themselves.
.PP
The ref is not core state. A clone that has never fetched it reads no checks
and reports none failing, which is the correct reading of what it holds rather
than a claim that the build passed.
.SH THE TWO LEGS
.B pull
and
.B push
each have two, and they write to different places.
.PP
The git leg carries three refs between clones: the reviews, the checks, and the
origin ledger. The ledger goes first in both directions \(em a clone that
received reviews without their mappings would re-file every one of them
upstream on its next bridge push, and nothing undoes that. Nothing is ever
forced: every one of these refs is a grow-only set of lines, so a refused push
is answered by pulling and pushing again, and no write is lost either way.
.PP
The bridge leg talks to a forge's API. A push prints its plan, asks before it
writes anything, and journals each mapping it earns as it earns it \(em so an
interrupted run is resumed rather than repeated. It reads the tracker's current
state by replaying the importer against it, which is what makes
.B --dry-run
exact rather than approximate.
.PP
Some things a pull request will not take, and each is reported per field rather
than dropped:
.BR closes ,
because GitHub derives closing links from keywords in the description (on Azure
DevOps the link is a separate artifact link, not written yet);
.BR status.reason ,
because a pull request has no close reason; a status of
.BR merged ,
because a merge is performed upstream; and
.B head
or
.BR head.sha ,
because a tracker learns those from the git remote. The Azure DevOps push also
reports reviewer and milestone changes unsent, and posts a verdict's message as
its own discussion thread since a vote carries no body. A review this clone
opened can only be filed once its branch is on the remote, and pushing the
branch stays an ordinary
.B git push
that this command will not do behind your back.
.SH CLOSES DOES NOT CLOSE
.B --closes
records that a review is meant to close an issue. It records intent and nothing
more: nothing here closes that issue, now or on merge. An issue's status
changes when an event on the issue's own blob says so.
.PP
Two reasons, and the first is structural. A cascade cannot converge \(em
folding one blob would depend on another's folded state, so a clone holding the
review but not the issue would fold a different answer from the same data. And
the link is not always a mandate: a review merged in a fork should not close an
issue upstream.
.PP
.B git review
never writes to
.BR refs/notes/issues/* .
.SH FILES
.TP
.B refs/notes/reviews/open
Reviews in any non-terminal status.
.TP
.B refs/notes/reviews/archived
Reviews that have reached a terminal one.
.TP
.B refs/notes/checks/runs
Check runs, keyed by commit sha.
.TP
.B refs/git-issue/origins
Which upstream object each entity is, per tracker.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR git-issue (1),
.BR git-notes (1),
.BR git-log (1)
.PP
The specification is in the repository's
.B docs/
directory:
.B reviews.md
for this type's vocabulary,
.B blob-format.md
for what one entity is,
.B storage-model.md
for where the bytes live.
